    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Lawrence Butler** (73.92) and **Jung Hoo Lee** (68.02) emerge as our premier offensive engines. **Lawrence Butler** benefits from a massive 13% boost due to his platoon advantage against the right-handed Freddy Peralta, paired with a favorable hitter\u2019s park that elevates his ceiling well beyond his base projection. Similarly, **Jung Hoo Lee** is an elite play, leveraging a strong matchup against Hayden Wesneski; the combination of a favorable wind direction and a top-two lineup spot provides the necessary volume to secure his high scoring status. **Bryce Harper** (62.86) and **Jonathan Aranda** (63.73) round out our tier of \"Strong\" starts, with **Jonathan Aranda** notably vaulting into this bracket despite a slight platoon penalty, thanks to a soft matchup against the 5.06 xERA of Jacob Lopez.\n\nThe most difficult decision on today\u2019s card was keeping **Kyle Karros** (42.87) on the bench. He hovered near the \"Marginal\" threshold, but ultimately the optimizer favored the higher lineup placement and situational efficiency of our active core. While **Kyle Karros** holds some intrigue against Michael Soroka, the negative SP skill variance of facing a pitcher with a 3.62 xERA hampered his outlook. Meanwhile, we remain cautious regarding our \"Lineup Pending\" designations; specifically, **Justin Crawford** (50.94) and **Yainer Diaz** (51.18) are currently tethered to the bottom of their respective batting orders. While both are active, these suboptimal spots depress their overall efficiency. We will continue to monitor lineups for **Dylan Beavers** and **Daulton Varsho** to ensure their projected spots in the middle-to-bottom of the order hold firm, particularly with **Daulton Varsho** getting a nice 10% boost from his platoon dynamic despite the risk of an elite catcher like the one behind the dish for Blade Tidwell.",
